== Structural audit ==

All 17 Core pages use [[Template:Knowledge object]], [[Template:Reading depth]], source status, references, controlled Domain values, and Core-owned category/semantic identity.

All 34 Simple/Technical derivatives use [[Template:Reading depth]], declare source status, contain sources/references, and do not duplicate Core Knowledge object/category identity.

== Title / identity audit ==

Qualified titles remain necessary for [[Protocol (computing)]] and [[Federation (information technology)]]. [[Uniform Resource Identifier]] remains canonical rather than using URL as a broader synonym. [[Application programming interface]] remains the full canonical title.

No vendor, website, or commercial platform is promoted into the primitive layer.

== Domain audit ==

No new controlled Domain is required. Wave 13 uses Computer science, Technology, Engineering, and Systems science.

== Internet / Web audit ==

The corpus explicitly preserves:

<pre>
Internet ≠ World Wide Web
Internet protocol suite ≠ Internet
DNS ≠ Web
URI ≠ location necessarily
HTTP ≠ HTML
HTTP ≠ Web
</pre>

The Web is treated as an information architecture using identifiers, representations, agents, and protocols over network infrastructure.

== Software-role audit ==

[[Client-server model]] treats client and server as interaction roles rather than permanent device identities.

[[Web browser]] remains distinct from a search engine.

[[Web server]] is a software/service role and is not reduced to a physical host.

[[Application programming interface]] remains broader than Web APIs and distinct from protocol as a general concept.

== Identity / access audit ==

[[Authentication]] and [[Authorization]] remain separate:

<pre>
authentication = establish confidence in a claimant/account/credential binding
authorization = determine allowed actions under policy
</pre>

Authentication is not treated as real-world identity proofing in every system, and authorization can exist for anonymous or capability-bearing requests.

== Federation / interoperability audit ==

[[Interoperability]] does not require identical implementations.

[[Federation (information technology)]] requires separately administered systems plus shared exchange/trust mechanisms; it is not equated with decentralization or single sign-on.

NIST SP 800-63 Revision 4 is cited as final July 2025 guidance. W3C ActivityPub is cited as a Recommendation dated 23 January 2018.

== Platform / moderation audit ==

[[Online platform]] is a sociotechnical service layer, not the Internet or a protocol.

[[Content moderation]] is modeled as policy application and enforcement. A moderation action is not treated as proof that content is false, harmful, illegal, or otherwise invalid outside the governing policy context.

== Link-resolution audit ==

Two draft related-concept links were rejected during Phase 3:

* <code>[[Policy]]</code> — no canonical page exists; kept as ordinary text.
* <code>[[Network effect]]</code> — no canonical page exists; kept as ordinary text.

No stubs were created merely to satisfy those links.

== Cross-wave audit ==

Wave 13 reuses [[Computer network]], [[Distributed system]], [[Computer security]], [[Identity]], [[Governance]], [[Semantic Web]], [[Human-computer interaction]], [[Machine learning]], and other established identities without redefining them.

No blockchain-specific identity or Web-specific application is allowed to redefine the general Internet/platform substrate.

